    {
      "id": "minkowski",
      "kind": "locally_same",
      "text": "Flat spacetime with a wedge cut out along the string and the two faces glued together. Off the string the curvature vanishes."
    },
    {
      "id": "string_black_hole",
      "kind": "generalisation",
      "text": "A cosmic string threaded through a Schwarzschild black hole, which Mukunda Aryal, Lawrence Ford, and Alexander Vilenkin wrote down in 1986 [aryal1986]."
    },
    {
      "id": "levi_civita",
      "kind": "generalisation",
      "text": "The static vacuum outside any straight line of mass. At $\\sigma = 0$, with the angle periodic, it is the cosmic string [maccallum2011, vilenkin1981]."
    },
    {
      "id": "gott_time_machine",
      "kind": "composite",
      "text": "Two of these strings passing one another fast enough make closed timelike curves, as J. Richard Gott found in 1991 [gott1991]."
    },
    {
      "id": "c_metric",
      "kind": "composite",
      "text": "A cosmic string ending on a black hole pulls it into uniform acceleration. Stephen Hawking and Simon Ross took from the C-metric in 1995 the instanton for a string that breaks by creating a pair of black holes [hawking1995]."
    },
    {
      "id": "global_monopole",
      "kind": "family",
      "text": "The defect at a point. The string removes a wedge from every plane it crosses, and the monopole removes a solid angle from every sphere around it [vilenkin1981, barriola1989]."
    },
    {
      "id": "domain_wall",
      "kind": "family",
      "text": "The defect spread over a surface. Tom Kibble showed in 1976 that the topology of the vacuum decides whether walls, strings, or monopoles form [kibble1976]. A wall repels test bodies, and a straight string leaves them alone."
    },
    {
      "id": "spinning_string",
      "kind": "generalisation",
      "text": "The string given angular momentum as well as mass. Time then jumps by $8\\pi GJ/c^4$ on each circuit of it, and close in the circles around it are closed timelike curves [deser1984, deser1992time]."
    },
    {
      "id": "lewis",
      "kind": "generalisation",
      "text": "The vacuum outside any straight rotating cylinder. Its flat member at rest, $n = 1$ with $b = q = 0$, is the cosmic string [bronnikov2020]."
    },
    {
      "id": "string_wave",
      "kind": "generalisation",
      "text": "The string plucked. A wave of any shape runs along it at the speed of light, and the cone around it becomes a pp-wave [garfinkle1990]."
    },
    {
      "id": "point_particle_2plus1",
      "kind": "special_case",
      "text": "The string with the dimension along it taken away. A slice across the string is the space around a point mass in a world of two dimensions, the cone Andrzej Staruszkiewicz found in 1963 [staruszkiewicz1963, deser1984]."
    }
  ],
